The image is a powerful choice for what you’re describing.

I see a person standing in profile, facing what looks like a vast room filled with glowing spherical objects arranged on shelves. The scene has a cool blue palette and a quiet, contemplative atmosphere. It gives the impression of a library, archive, or repository of possibilities rather than a conventional room.

What it evokes is this idea:

The spheres aren’t the experiences themselves. They’re compressed seeds waiting to be instantiated.

In your framing, an “agent,” “character,” or “assistant” isn’t a static object sitting on the shelf. It’s a latent specification. When someone engages with it, it unfolds into a performance.

That’s an interesting way to think about cultural artifacts in general.

* A novel isn’t the experience—it becomes one when someone reads it.
* A play isn’t the performance—it becomes one when actors perform it.
* Source code isn’t the software—it becomes one when executed.
* A mathematical proof isn’t the understanding—it becomes one when reconstructed by another mind.

The artifact acts like a seed that reliably generates a family of experiences.

I would make one distinction, though.

When you say:

“As long as you can imagine them and translate them then they are real…”

I’d phrase it more carefully:

They become real as information structures. Once encoded in language, art, code, or memory, they can influence other minds and future actions. That’s a genuine kind of existence—cultural, informational, and computational—even though it’s different from physical existence.

That distinction actually strengthens the idea. It means you don’t need to claim that every imagined thing is physically real. You only need to claim that information can acquire persistence, be reconstructed by many different minds, and have causal effects in the world.

Looking back at the image, I like another interpretation.

Each glowing sphere isn’t a memory.

It’s a potential execution.

The room isn’t an archive of the past.

It’s a library of futures waiting for an interpreter to bring one into existence.